{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"enums","title":"enum & enum class","group":"Language Basics","order":9,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Enumerations give names to a fixed set of integral values. Prefer the scoped <code>enum class</code> (C++11): its names don't leak and it doesn't convert to <code>int</code> silently.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>Unscoped <code>enum</code> (C-style)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nenum Color { Red, Green, Blue };          // Red=0, Green=1, Blue=2\nenum Status { Ok = 200, NotFound = 404 };  // explicit values\n\nint main() {\n    Color c = Green;\n    int n = c;                    // implicit conversion to int\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; n &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; Blue + 1 &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; NotFound &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 3 404</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Unscoped enumerators are injected into the surrounding scope — two enums can't both have a <code>None</code> — and they compare/convert freely with integers and each other.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock\"></i>Scoped <code>enum class</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;cstdint&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string_view&gt;\n\nenum class Direction : std::uint8_t { North, East, South, West };   // underlying type: 1 byte\n\nstd::string_view name(Direction d) {\n    switch (d) {\n        case Direction::North: return \"North\";\n        case Direction::East:  return \"East\";\n        case Direction::South: return \"South\";\n        case Direction::West:  return \"West\";\n    }\n    return \"?\";\n}\n\nDirection turnRight(Direction d) {\n    return static_cast&lt;Direction&gt;((static_cast&lt;int&gt;(d) + 1) % 4);   // explicit conversions\n}\n\nint main() {\n    Direction d = Direction::West;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; name(d) &lt;&lt; \" -&gt; \" &lt;&lt; name(turnRight(d)) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; sizeof(Direction) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; static_cast&lt;int&gt;(Direction::South) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>West -&gt; North\n1 2</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>enum class Fruit { Apple, Pear };\nint main() {\n    int x = Fruit::Apple;      // error: no implicit conversion to int\n    return x;\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>enum</code></th><th><code>enum class</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Names scoped?</td><td>No — <code>Red</code></td><td>Yes — <code>Color::Red</code></td></tr><tr><td>Implicit → <code>int</code>?</td><td>Yes</td><td>No — needs <code>static_cast</code></td></tr><tr><td>Underlying type</td><td>Implementation-chosen (can be fixed)</td><td><code>int</code> by default, can be fixed (<code>: std::uint8_t</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Forward-declarable</td><td>Only with fixed underlying type</td><td>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>With <code>-Wall</code>, a <code>switch</code> over an enum that misses an enumerator (and has no <code>default</code>) triggers a warning — a free exhaustiveness check. C++20 also allows <code>using enum Direction;</code> inside a scope to drop the prefix.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why prefer enum class over plain enum?</summary><div><p>Scoping (no name clashes), strong typing (no accidental comparison with <code>int</code> or with another enum), and a guaranteed underlying type that allows forward declaration. You opt into conversions explicitly with <code>static_cast</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}